WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.520
There are Teslas everywhere in this
city.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:16.060
Up until today I'd seen a total of about five Teslas my entire life.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:19.080
And three of those five were behind glass

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.040
cases in luxury malls.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:25.980
I've been in Oslo
for like a couple of hours and I've seen like 50 Teslas.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.060
There's one right there.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.320
They're beautiful, they're sleek, they are

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.900
efficient, they're fast and they're
$70,000.

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:37.860
Here comes another one up the road.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.320
Oh yeah.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:49.380
100% electricity, no gas, and it
can go from like zero to 100 kilometers

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.700
in like five seconds. I have no idea, I'm not
a car person, so I don't know if that's right.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.739
Here in Norway in 2014,
Tesla actually broke a record for number

00:01:01.739 --> 00:01:06.180
of cars sold in a month for a single
model, of any kind of car not just

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.479
electric cars. And you look around for a
few minutes and you realize it's not

00:01:09.479 --> 00:01:16.350
just Teslas. There are electric cars
everywhere. Like, I see electric cars in

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.710
the United States but nothing like this.
Oh, there's a Tesla, there's a Tesla.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:20.700
There it goes.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.500
Turns out that Oslo is
actually the epicenter of electric cars generally.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:37.580
You can tell which cars are electric by
their license plate. So this "E" at the

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:43.159
beginning determines if it's electric.
In 2016 in the United States of all the new

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:49.300
cars that were purchased about 1% were
electric, more like 0.9%.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:57.180
In Norway it was 29% and that trend is only speeding up:
in January this year the share was like

00:01:57.180 --> 00:02:02.120
37% or something of all the cars
purchased being electric.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:09.200
Where on earth have I ended up? I've
been kind of wandering around and I feel

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.470
like I'm about to enter a secret cave.
The real question is can I fly my drone

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:20.280
over this amazing waterfall? I've never flown
a drone in Norway before so we'll see how this goes.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:45.260
99% of Norway's electricity comes from
hydropower which is like dams.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:50.760
So you have really cheap clean energy that can
be used to power these electric cars.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:03:00.440
The real core explanation here, the reason why
there are so many Teslas and so many

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:04.140
electric cars on the road comes down to
government policy.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:09.120
The government incentivizes very very strongly people
to buy electric cars.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.330
This happens in various forms: you get
free parking if you drive an electric

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:17.840
car, you get access to the HOV Lane, which
has way less traffic,

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:24.260
you don't have to pay registration fees, you get tax
deductions on your income tax,

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:28.500
and perhaps the biggest incentive of all is
on the business side: companies like

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:33.180
Tesla don't ha ve to pay sales tax for
selling here in Norway and so all of

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:39.360
this combined makes it actually way way
cheaper for someone to buy a Tesla here

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:42.180
in Norway than to buy one in the United
States.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:46.140
Well it's been fun exploring this random
little area I think the big takeaway

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:51.600
from this whole entire journey so far is
even thugz cry.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.300
So just remember: even thugz cry.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:03.340
Alright, this is perhaps my favorite sight so far.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:07.660
There are 2,000 of these charging stations here in Oslo.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:13.500
Excuse me can I ask you a couple, I'm so
curious about - can I record it? Yeah, of course.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:17.220
So it's it's all free. It's free? Yeah, it's free.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:22.460
This is what you get from buying an
electric car. You get free charging and

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:29.100
you can go in and out of Oslo without
paying for the "bompenger" (toll).

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:49.720
All of these subsidies we're talking about that,
help fund these electric cars are funded

00:04:49.729 --> 00:04:55.910
by what's called sovereign wealth fund.
It's this huge fund that Norway has it's

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:59.539
worth almost a trillion dollars. The
government owns it it's basically their

00:04:59.540 --> 00:05:04.800
rainy day fund and this is what they use
to fund the subsidies.

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:09.720
The sovereign wealth fund in Norway is comprised
almost entirely of oil money,

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:16.039
oil and gas money, fossil fuels. Norway's a huge
producer of oil and gas and they sell

00:05:16.039 --> 00:05:21.320
that to other countries and reap the
benefits in terms of revenue and put

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:24.949
that into the sovereign wealth fund
which then gets funneled into subsidies

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:29.060
for electric cars. So they're not burning
the oil here and releasing carbon into

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:32.240
the atmosphere but they're sending off
to go be burned somewhere else.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:38.360
I asked my friend Tor what he thinks about this.
Tor, what do you feel about Norway being

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:43.849
this green sustainable society, but
really depending at the end of the day

00:05:43.849 --> 00:05:45.770
on fossil fuels in order to make that
happen?

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:52.280
Is that a tension within the Norwegian
psyche? So I think the way we resolve it

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:59.240
is basically that the world needs oil.
It needs, you know there's a lot of

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:03.050
countries are developing: China, India,
that will always need energy and we

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:07.880
would argue that we extract it more
greenly than other countries.

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:11.870
We're buying good conscience for
sure yeah but like it's better than

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:16.100
doing anything. We're fully aware that
there's like green shift we're talking

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:22.040
about is funded by you know oil money.
The irony is not lost on us in any way.

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:26.300
So I think Tor is right -- I think that the
fact that Norway is exporting its carbon

00:06:26.300 --> 00:06:31.120
footprint to other countries, it doesn't
cancel out all of the wonderful

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.980
progressive green things that they're
doing here.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:40.900
This is a story about incentives and green infrastructure and a green society but it's also a story

00:06:40.910 --> 00:06:45.280
about how fossil fuels continue to make
the world go round.

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:50.150
I am tired and jet-lagged and haven't
slept in like twenty three hours.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:56.820
I'm gonna go to bed. It was a fun day chasing Teslas. Tomorrow, we go to the North Pole.

